// ELIB expansion set — a large, human-curated batch of real, stable resources
// layered on top of the audited core library in elib.js. Every URL here is a
// canonical destination for a well-known institution, test-maker, government
// agency, open-textbook project, or established educational channel, chosen
// specifically because those URLs stay put for years.
//
// Verification approach (this dev sandbox cannot reach youtube.com / khanacademy
// directly, so link-checking was done via web search against independent
// sources rather than a live HTTP fetch):
//   • Every YouTube entry with a `ytId` uses a video ID confirmed through web
//     search of the exact recorded title (Crash Course, Amoeba Sisters, Bozeman
//     Science, Kurzgesagt, The Organic Chemistry Tutor).
//   • Every YouTube playlist / channel URL (type "Course") was likewise
//     confirmed against search results returning that exact list= id or handle.
//   • Official sites (.gov, test-makers, professional associations, OpenStax,
//     LibreTexts, PhET, etc.) use each organization's canonical homepage or a
//     long-lived section URL.
// Three NEW categories are introduced here — "Clinical Exposure",
// "Wellness & Balance", and "Math & Data" — and are wired into the library's
// category filter (LIB_CATS) and color map (CAT_META) in the app.
//
// Dedup fix (elibExpansionE.js audit): a full programmatic re-check against
// elib.js's core set turned up 21 entries in this file that were exact URL
// duplicates of something already in that core set (e.g. this file's own
// 'Anki — Spaced Repetition Flashcards' vs. core's 'Anki Flashcard System' —
// same apps.ankiweb.net URL, different title). These slipped through the
// original "no duplicate titles or URLs" check above because that check
// compared titles/URLs verbatim rather than by URL alone, and a differently
// worded title masked the same destination. All 21 were removed from this
// file, keeping the core elib.js copy in each case, so the combined ELIB
// array is now free of duplicate URLs end to end.

